course: adult-life
lesson: 7
title: Cards, banks and apps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most grown-ups keep their money in a bank account, and most of it is never seen as notes and coins. It moves as numbers when a card is tapped, a bill goes out or pay comes in.
explain: A debit card spends money already in the account; a credit card borrows money that must be paid back later. A PIN is the secret number that proves the card is yours.
explain: Banks pay interest on savings, and they charge interest when you borrow. Interest that grows on interest is called compound interest.
explain: Example: in a café in Toronto, Mum taps her phone on the reader and the coffee is paid in a second. That night she opens her banking app and groans: "The app says we've spent eighty dollars on takeaways this month."
explain: The money was real every time, it just did not feel like it, which is why grown-ups check their statements and apps.
fact: Contactless means paying by touching a card or phone to a reader, and real money leaves the account even though no notes change hands.
fact: A PIN is a secret four-digit number that proves a card is yours, and banks never ask for it on the phone.
fact: A debit card spends money already in the account; a credit card borrows money from the card company to be paid back later.
fact: An overdraft lets you spend below zero, which is borrowing, and banks charge high interest on it.
fact: A savings account pays interest, a little extra money each year for every pound kept there, because the bank lends your money to others.
fact: Compound interest means you earn interest on the interest, so savings grow faster over many years, and unpaid debts grow the same way.
fact: Budgeting apps sort spending into groups like food and fun, so people see where the money really goes.
fact: A bank statement lists everything that went in and out of an account, and grown-ups check it for mistakes and fraud.
fact: A subscription keeps taking money every month until it is cancelled, and free trials often turn into payments.
fact: Some cash machines charge a fee for taking out money, while most bank ones are free.
fact: Banks offer children's accounts to keep savings safe and teach how banks work.
fact: Cashback at a shop means taking your own money out as cash while paying by card; it is not a gift.
exercise: Mum says "Just tap your card, it's contactless." What happens when you tap? | Real money leaves the bank account at once | The shop gives the item for free if you tap fast enough | The card earns money for the family | Nothing is paid until next year
exercise: Why is it easy to overspend with contactless? | You don't see any notes or coins go | The reader always charges you double the price | Contactless payments are never checked | The bank quietly adds extra money each tap
exercise: Dad says "Never tell anyone your PIN." Why not? | Anyone with the card and the PIN can take the money | The PIN changes by itself each time someone else hears it | Saying it aloud makes the card stop working | Only the shop is allowed to know it
exercise: Someone rings saying "I'm from your bank, tell me your PIN." What is true? | Banks never ask for a PIN on the phone | Banks do ask, so tell them quickly | Tell them only the first two digits | Tell them if they know your name
exercise: "Is that the debit card or the credit card?" What is the difference? | Debit spends your money; credit borrows money to pay back | Debit is for shops; credit is only for the internet | Debit borrows money; credit spends what you have | They are the same card with two names
exercise: Mum says "We're in our overdraft." What does it mean? | The account has gone below zero, which is borrowing | The account has more money in it than it has ever had | The bank has closed the account for good | The family has paid off the mortgage
exercise: Dad says "My savings pay three pounds a year for every hundred." What happens to £100 in a year? | It becomes £103 | It goes down to £97 | It jumps to £300 | It stays exactly £100
exercise: Why does a bank pay interest on savings? | It lends your money to other people | It is required by law to give money away | The government pays it on the bank's behalf | The coins in the vault grow in number over time
exercise: Grandad says "Interest on interest, that's the magic." What does he mean? | Savings earn interest on earlier interest, so they grow faster | Interest is paid twice as a bonus every year to loyal customers | Interest is only paid on magic accounts | The bank forgets to take any fees
exercise: Mum says "The app says we've spent eighty pounds on takeaways this month." What does the app do? | It sorts spending into groups to show where money goes | It orders takeaways automatically every week for the family | It stops the family from spending at all | It gives back eighty pounds as a reward
exercise: Dad says "Let me check the bank statement." What is on it? | Everything that went in and out of the account | Only the money the family saved this year in the bank | A list of shops that are cheaper | The bank's rules for children
exercise: "That subscription's still taking money? I forgot we had it." What is a subscription? | A regular payment that continues until it is cancelled | A one-off payment that never repeats | A gift that a company sends for free to its best customers | A fee that stops by itself after a month
exercise: "This cash machine wants to charge two pounds fifty." What should a grown-up do? | Find a free machine, since small fees add up | Pay it, since all machines charge that | Take out more cash to make it worth it | Press yes quickly before it changes
exercise: Mum says "I've opened a children's bank account for you." Why is that useful? | Your savings are safe and may earn a little interest | The bank will give you pocket money each week as a present | You can now borrow as much as you like | Your money will double every month
exercise: Dad at the till: "Can I have twenty pounds cashback?" Where does the twenty pounds come from? | His own bank account | The shop, as a free gift | The shop's charity box | A loan from the cashier
exercise: A game offers a "free trial" that asks for a card. What should you do? | Ask a grown-up first, as it may turn into payments | Start it, because free trials are always free and stay free | Use a friend's card instead | Type in any card number you can find
test: Your brother says "Contactless isn't real money, it's just beeping." Put him right. | It is real money taken straight from the account | He is right, it is only practice money | It is the shop's money, not ours | It only becomes real if you sign
test: A message pops up: "Bank security! Reply with your card PIN to unlock your account." What do you do? | Show a grown-up and never reply with the PIN | Reply quickly so the account unlocks before it is too late | Reply with only half of the PIN | Send the PIN but not the card number
test: Auntie laughs: "Credit card's great, it's free money!" Which is true? | It is a loan, with a bill on the way | It is free money that never needs paying | It only works for buying presents | The bank pays the bill if you forget
test: Dad on the phone, stressed: "We're £200 in the red till payday." What is happening? | The account is below zero, in the overdraft | The family has £200 saved up | The bank owes the family £200 | Payday has been cancelled this month
test: Grandad puts £1000 in savings and leaves it there 30 years, never taking the interest out. What happens? | It grows faster and faster as interest earns interest | It stays at £1000 because savings never grow | It grows by the same small amount, and no more, every year | It shrinks every year because the bank takes big fees
test: Mum scrolls the statement and gasps "What's this £9.99 every month since March??" What has probably happened? | A forgotten subscription kept taking money | The bank has given her a present each month | Someone paid her back for a takeaway | The cash machine charged her once
test: Your mate says "That cash machine says £2.50 fee, but whatever, it's only once." Why might a grown-up still walk on? | Small fees add up over a year | The fee is only charged to children | Free machines give out extra money | The fees all go straight to charity
test: Your sister writes down her pocket money spending for a month and is shocked how much went on sweets. What is she doing? | The same as a budgeting app does for grown-ups | Making the shop lower its prices | Earning interest on her sweets | Opening a credit card account
test: Dad: "Need cash for the window cleaner, I'll get cashback at Tesco." What will he do? | Take his own cash out while paying for shopping | Ask the shop to pay the window cleaner | Get a free twenty pounds from the shop | Borrow money from the cashier
test: Nan says "Keep your money under the mattress, banks are useless." What is a good reply? | A bank keeps it safer and may pay interest | Money under a mattress earns more interest than a bank | Banks always charge you for keeping savings safe | Banks spend children's savings on their own staff
test: Your cousin rang the bank and says "they said they'd never ask for my PIN, so the caller was lying?" Is he right? | Yes, banks never ask for a PIN on the phone | No, banks ask for it every month | No, banks only ask on Sundays | Yes, but the caller could still be trusted
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the distinction between a debit card and a credit card? | The former draws upon existing funds; the latter constitutes borrowing | The former constitutes borrowing; the latter draws upon existing funds | The two instruments are functionally identical in every respect | The former is valid solely for purchases made online
exam: It is given that a sum of one hundred pounds is deposited at interest of three pounds per hundred per annum. Ascertain the balance at the conclusion of one year, no withdrawals having been made. | One hundred and three pounds | Ninety-seven pounds | Three hundred pounds | One hundred pounds
exam: Identify the conduct appropriate where a caller purporting to represent a bank requests disclosure of a personal identification number. | Disclosure must be refused, as banks never request it | Disclosure is permitted provided the caller correctly states the account name | Partial disclosure of the first two digits is permitted | Disclosure is required to prevent account closure
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning a recurring subscription. | Payments continue until the subscription is cancelled | Payments cease automatically after a single month has elapsed | The provider is obliged to refund all payments | It is settled exclusively in cash at the point of sale
